# 02 - VirtualBox Setup

## 1. Install VirtualBox

Install Oracle VirtualBox on the host system.

Official download:
https://www.virtualbox.org/wiki/Downloads

## 2. Create Kali Linux VM

### Recommended Configuration

- Name: Kali-Linux
- Type: Linux
- Version: Debian (64-bit)
- RAM: 4–8 GB
- CPU: 2–4 cores
- Storage: 30–40 GB
- Network Adapter 1: NAT

### Installation

1. Create a new virtual machine.
2. Select the downloaded Kali Linux ISO.
3. Allocate the recommended resources.
4. Complete the Kali Linux installation.
5. Verify that Kali boots successfully.

---

## 3. Create Windows VM

### Recommended Configuration

- Name: Win11-LAB
- Type: Microsoft Windows
- Version: Windows 11 (64-bit)
- RAM: 4 GB minimum
- CPU: 2–4 cores
- Storage: ~30 GB minimum for this basic lab
- Network Adapter 1: NAT

### Installation

1. Create a new virtual machine.
2. Select the Windows ISO.
3. Allocate the recommended resources.
4. Complete the Windows installation.
5. Verify that Windows boots successfully.

---

## 4. Network Adapters

After both operating systems are installed, configure a second
network adapter on each VM.

### Adapter 1

- Attached to: NAT
- Purpose: Internet connectivity

### Adapter 2

- Attached to: Internal Network
- Network Name: `SOC-LAB`
- Purpose: Isolated communication between lab VMs

The detailed configuration of the internal network is covered in:

[03-Network-Configuration](../03-Network-Configuration/README.md)

---

## 5. VM Storage

Store the virtual machines on a drive with sufficient free space.

Dynamic virtual disks can grow as the guest operating system uses more
storage, so the host system must have enough free space available.

---

## 6. Verification

Before continuing:

- [ ] Kali boots successfully
- [ ] Windows boots successfully
- [ ] Both VMs have sufficient allocated resources
- [ ] Adapter 1 is configured as NAT
- [ ] Adapter 2 is configured as an Internal Network
- [ ] Both VMs use the same Internal Network name
